IMPLEMENTATION DOCUMENT
Design Thinking World — Location-Based 8-Step Story + PDF Export
Phaser 3 (CDN) — Procedural Tilemap + DOM UI
Last updated: 2026-04-11


0. Quick Start (Run the Game)

Requirements
  - A modern browser (Chrome/Edge recommended)
  - Python (for local static server)
  - Internet connection (Phaser + jsPDF are loaded from CDNs in index.html)

Run locally
  1) Open a terminal in the project root (d:\Programs\DTPB)
  2) Start a server:
       python -m http.server 8000
  3) Open in browser:
       http://localhost:8000
  4) Stop the server with Ctrl+C

Controls
  - Move: WASD or Arrow Keys
  - Interact / confirm: E or Space


1. Project Overview

This project is a Phaser 3 educational game that guides a student through a fixed, sequential 8-step design thinking story.
The main gameplay happens in a “village hub” world. The player must walk to the next unlocked location on the map.
When the player interacts at that location, the task opens as either:
  - a popup task panel (DOM overlay), or
  - a separate mini-game scene (Football and Maze)

All text answers and drawings are stored in a global game state object and exported at the end as a multi-page PDF report.

Key requirements implemented
  - Location-driven progression (only the next unlocked location is active)
  - Mixed UX (some tasks are panels, some are separate scenes)
  - Persistent in-session storage of all task data (text + drawings)
  - Final export to PDF including drawings, multi-page, with auto-download + manual download button


2. Tech Stack & Dependencies

Core
  - Phaser 3 (loaded via CDN)
  - JavaScript in browser (no bundler; scripts loaded globally via <script> tags)

UI
  - Phaser DOM Elements (dom: { createContainer: true }) for popup forms

Export
  - jsPDF (loaded via CDN) for PDF report generation

State & Events
  - Global state object: src/data/gameState.js
  - Global event emitter: src/utils/EventBus.js


3. Project Layout (Important Files)

Entry
  - index.html
      Loads Phaser + jsPDF from CDNs, then loads all game scripts in the correct order.
  - main.js
      Builds Phaser config and creates the Phaser.Game instance.

Scenes
  - src/scenes/BootScene.js
  - src/scenes/CharSelectScene.js
  - src/scenes/GameScene.js
  - src/scenes/UIScene.js
  - src/scenes/FootballScene.js
  - src/scenes/MazeScene.js
  - src/scenes/CompleteScene.js

Game objects & UI
  - src/objects/Player.js
  - src/objects/NPC.js
  - src/ui/HUD.js
  - src/ui/TaskPanel.js
  - src/ui/ShopPanel.js

Data
  - src/data/tasks.js (task definitions + TASK_ORDER)
  - src/data/gameState.js (points, completion, answers/drawings storage)
  - src/data/upgrades.js (upgrade catalogue)

Procedural generation
  - src/scripts/TilesetGenerator.js (generates tiles + Tiled-style map JSON + Zones)
  - src/scripts/SpriteGenerator.js (generates player/NPC spritesheets)

Note
  - scripts/build-world.js currently exists but is empty.


4. Boot & Scene Flow

4.1 Scene list (registered in main.js)
  - BootScene
  - CharSelectScene
  - GameScene
  - UIScene (runs in parallel with GameScene)
  - FootballScene (Task 2)
  - MazeScene (Task 5)
  - CompleteScene

4.2 Runtime flow
  1) BootScene
      - Preloads required external images (buildings/props + spritesheets)
      - Generates the tileset and tilemap JSON procedurally
      - Generates character spritesheets and utility textures
      - Creates animations
      - Starts CharSelectScene
  2) CharSelectScene
      - Sets gameState.playerGender
      - Starts GameScene + UIScene
  3) GameScene
      - Builds the hub world, player, collisions, NPCs
      - Builds story Zones and enforces sequential unlocking
  4) UIScene
      - Shows HUD
      - Opens TaskPanel/ShopPanel when EventBus emits npc:interact
  5) FootballScene / MazeScene
      - Mini-game tasks
      - Save answers, award points, complete task, return to hub
  6) CompleteScene
      - Final congratulations view
      - Auto-download PDF once + provides a Download button


5. Map & World System (Procedural Tiled JSON)

5.1 Core constants
  - TILE_SIZE = 32
  - MAP_COLS = 40
  - MAP_ROWS = 30

5.2 Layers created (Tiled-style)
  - Ground (visual)
  - Walls (collision)
  - Objects (collision)
  - Buildings (kept for layer-index compatibility; not used for story progression)
  - Upgrade (tiles swapped by upgrades)
  - NPCs (objectgroup)
  - Spawn (objectgroup)
  - Zones (objectgroup; story locations)

5.3 High-resolution image objects
Instead of putting all buildings into a tile layer, TilesetGenerator creates a list of image objects (house1-house5, shop tent, decor)
and stores them in the Scene registry. GameScene reads these and instantiates them as images with physics collision zones.

5.4 Collision
  - Walls layer + Objects layer are collidable
  - Static image objects have custom collision bounds
  - Player uses a “legs-only” hitbox for better feel (Player.body size/offset)


6. Story Progression System (Zones)

6.1 Why Zones
The story is location-driven. Only the next unlocked story location should be active.
To achieve this, a dedicated Tiled object layer called "Zones" is generated and consumed by GameScene.

6.2 Zone object properties
Each zone rectangle contains properties:
  - taskId   (string, required)
  - label    (string, shown in on-screen prompt)
  - mode     ("panel" or "scene")
  - sceneKey (only for mode="scene")

6.3 Active task logic
  - TASK_ORDER defines the fixed order of tasks.
  - The active task is the first task in TASK_ORDER that is:
      unlocked AND not complete
  - Only the active task’s zone is actionable.

6.4 Interaction
  - Player emits scene event 'player:interact' when E or Space is pressed.
  - GameScene handles the event:
      a) if the player is inside the active Zone → trigger the Zone
      b) else interact with the nearest NPC (optional fallback)

6.5 On-screen prompt
When player is inside the active Zone and movement is enabled, a text prompt appears:
  “Press E: <Zone Label>”


7. Tasks (8-Step Story)

7.1 Task order
Defined in src/data/tasks.js:
  TASK_ORDER = [
    'task1',  // Home
    'task2',  // Playground football
    'task3a', // Classroom user profile + drawing
    'task3b', // Corridor sad/happy
    'task5',  // Maze
    'task6',  // Park ideation + drawing
    'task7',  // School validation
    'task4'   // Landmark final idea name
  ]

7.2 Task UI mode summary
  - Panel (TaskPanel): task1, task3a, task3b, task6, task7, task4
  - Scene (mini-game): task2 (FootballScene), task5 (MazeScene)

7.3 Storage rules (what gets saved)
For every task (panel or scene):
  - Raw answers are stored at:
      gameState.taskAnswers[taskId]

Drawings (when applicable):
  - task3a user drawing:
      gameState.userProfile.drawingData
      gameState.taskDrawings['task3a']
  - task3b sad/happy drawings:
      gameState.sadHappyData.sad.drawingData
      gameState.sadHappyData.happy.drawingData
      (also stored for convenience as taskDrawings['task3b_sad'] / ['task3b_happy'])
  - task6 idea drawing:
      gameState.taskDrawings['task6']

7.4 Points & completion
  - Each task has a points value in src/data/tasks.js
  - Some tasks award bonus points when a drawing is provided (task3a, task6)
  - Completion is tracked by gameState.tasksComplete


8. Global State (gameState)

File: src/data/gameState.js

Core fields
  - points
  - tasksComplete[]
  - upgradesPurchased[]
  - playerGender

Generic export-focused storage
  - taskAnswers: { [taskId]: { ...answers } }
  - taskDrawings: { [taskId or key]: dataUrl }

Structured fields used by panels/summary
  - problemStatement
  - empathyStory
  - userProfile
  - sadHappyData
  - finalStatement

Unlocking helpers
  - isTaskUnlocked(taskId): true if taskId is task1 OR previous task in TASK_ORDER is complete
  - allTasksComplete: true if every taskId in TASK_ORDER is complete


9. EventBus (Cross-Scene Events)

File: src/utils/EventBus.js

Important events
  - npc:interact
      Emitted by NPCs or Zones; UIScene listens and opens TaskPanel/ShopPanel.
  - panel:close
      Emitted when a panel closes; GameScene uses this to re-enable movement.
  - task:completed
      Emitted by gameState.completeTask(); HUD uses this to update progress.
  - points:changed / points:added
      Used by HUD and the floating “+points” effect.


10. UI System (HUD + Panels)

10.1 UIScene
UIScene runs in parallel with the world. It creates the HUD and opens panels based on EventBus events.
It ensures listeners are cleaned up on scene shutdown to prevent duplication after returning from mini-games.

10.2 HUD
File: src/ui/HUD.js
Shows:
  - total points
  - task progress (completed/total)
Updates via EventBus events.

10.3 TaskPanel
File: src/ui/TaskPanel.js
Implements multiple panel “templates” (task1, task3a, task3b, task4) plus a generic form builder.
Supports:
  - validation based on minLength/minWords rules
  - optional drawing canvas (single-canvas tasks)
  - sad/happy split drawing with pointer events
On submit:
  - stores answers in taskAnswers
  - stores drawings (if present)
  - awards points and completes the task

Note: Task 2 is completed in FootballScene. A legacy Task 2 panel builder still exists but is not used in the current zone-based flow.


11. Mini-Game Scenes

11.1 FootballScene (Task 2)
File: src/scenes/FootballScene.js
  - Player must touch each of 4 corner “goals”
  - Each goal opens a DOM prompt and stores an answer
  - After all 4 are answered:
      - saves answers into gameState.taskAnswers['task2']
      - awards points
      - completes the task and returns to the hub

11.2 MazeScene (Task 5)
File: src/scenes/MazeScene.js
  - Simple rectangle maze made from static wall rectangles
  - Player reaches 3 checkpoints
  - Each checkpoint opens a DOM prompt and stores an answer
  - After all 3 are answered:
      - saves answers into gameState.taskAnswers['task5']
      - awards points
      - completes the task and returns to the hub


12. PDF Export

File: src/scenes/CompleteScene.js

Trigger
  - Auto-download once when entering CompleteScene
  - Manual download via “Download PDF Report” button

Content
  - Title page:
      Date, total points, quick summary
  - One page per task in TASK_ORDER:
      - key/value text from gameState.taskAnswers[taskId]
      - drawings embedded when available

Implementation details
  - Uses jsPDF (window.jspdf.jsPDF) loaded from CDN in index.html
  - Adds pages automatically when content exceeds page height
  - Embeds images via addImage(dataUrl, 'PNG', ...)
  - Filename includes user name + date


13. Assets

13.1 Assets loaded from files (BootScene preload)
Buildings/props (PNG)
  - assets/craftpix-net-504452-free-village-pixel-tileset-for-top-down-defense/...

Spritesheets (PNG + fixed frame sizes)
  - assets/hotdogs.png   frameWidth=50,  frameHeight=45
  - assets/hugedogs.png  frameWidth=22,  frameHeight=18

Cow (PNG sheet)
  - assets/cow_sprite.png  sliced at runtime into cow_idle/cow_walk frames

Scenery sheet (PNG)
  - assets/Nateonus8x8PixelPack/FullSpriteSheetsNoPadding/Scenery/Grass&StoneScenery.png

13.2 Procedural assets (generated at runtime)
  - Tileset texture: generated by TilesetGenerator (Canvas)
  - Map JSON: generated by TilesetGenerator
  - Player/NPC spritesheets: generated by SpriteGenerator (Canvas)
  - Exclamation marker: generated by SpriteGenerator
  - Pet sheets: generated by SpriteGenerator


14. Customization Guide

Change story text / fields / points
  - Edit src/data/tasks.js

Change where each task happens (zone size/position)
  - Edit zoneObjects in src/scripts/TilesetGenerator.js
    (x, y, width, height are in pixels; usually written as tile*32)

Change which tasks open a popup panel vs a new scene
  - In zoneObjects, set mode='panel' or mode='scene'
  - For scene zones, set sceneKey to the Phaser scene key

Replace Football goal questions
  - Edit the goals array in src/scenes/FootballScene.js

Replace Maze checkpoint questions
  - Edit the checkpoints array in src/scenes/MazeScene.js

Add a new task (high-level steps)
  1) Add task definition to src/data/tasks.js
  2) Insert the new taskId into TASK_ORDER
  3) Add a new zone object in src/scripts/TilesetGenerator.js
  4) If it needs special UI, add panel logic in src/ui/TaskPanel.js OR create a new Scene file
  5) Ensure answers/drawings are stored into gameState.taskAnswers/taskDrawings for PDF export


15. Known Limitations / Notes

  - No persistence across page refresh (state is in-memory only).
  - Offline use requires replacing CDN dependencies (Phaser/jsPDF) with local copies.
  - Upgrade “spawn NPC / spawn pet” logic in GameScene may need alignment with current textures/constructor usage.
  - FootballScene and MazeScene questions are placeholders by default and should be replaced with final content.